WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.807
[Alexandrina] <i>Camera traps are used
by the conservation community</i>

00:00:03.933 --> 00:00:06.450
<i>to take inventory of wildlife populations</i>

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:09.632
<i>and ultimately measure
the health of biodiversity</i>

00:00:09.643 --> 00:00:13.046
<i>in a non-invasive way
using motion-sensor cameras.</i>

00:00:14.022 --> 00:00:17.076
<i>These cameras take care
of automatically snapping photos</i>

00:00:17.211 --> 00:00:18.791
<i>when movement is detected.</i>

00:00:19.158 --> 00:00:21.135
<i>But, the tricky part after is reviewing</i>

00:00:21.135 --> 00:00:24.854
<i>each of the millions of photos
and counting each species</i>

00:00:24.854 --> 00:00:27.654
<i>as well as bad actors such as poachers.</i>

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.120
[camera clicks]

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.867
This is where machine
learning is of great help.

00:00:32.234 --> 00:00:34.097
And so in this episode, we will share

00:00:34.097 --> 00:00:37.659
how to build an image classification
app for camera traps.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:39.500
So let's dive in.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.260
♪ [music] ♪

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:46.906
<i>Thanks to data,</i>

00:00:46.906 --> 00:00:48.968
<i>today, we can understand our natural world</i>

00:00:48.968 --> 00:00:51.160
<i>with far greater richness
than ever before</i>

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:53.274
<i>by orders of magnitude.</i>

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:56.207
<i>This now empowers us to build amazing apps</i>

00:00:56.207 --> 00:01:00.450
<i>that can help us solve complex social
and environmental challenges.</i>

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.870
And so in this series,
let's build an app together.

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.180
<i>Meet Tanya from Google,
where her and a team of volunteers</i>

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.401
<i>have done an amazing job
at partnering with multiple organizations</i>

00:01:11.401 --> 00:01:14.760
<i>to launch a successful image
classifier for camera traps</i>

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.499
<i>along with a web app that lets
anyone upload camera trap data</i>

00:01:18.689 --> 00:01:22.330
<i>since it's fundamental
in machine learning to use a lot of data.</i>

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:24.780
And so sharing with as many organizations

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.028
is essential to research
as many species as possible.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.886
You can check out the website
at <i>WildlifeInsights.org</i>

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.806
Wildlife Insights is a project
that's a collaboration

00:01:33.806 --> 00:01:37.183
between leading conservation
organizations and Google.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.827
And the bigger picture challenge
that we're facing

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:45.493
is we've lost 68%
of wildlife populations on average

00:01:45.493 --> 00:01:46.997
since the 1970s.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.601
<i>Biologists, for a number of years,</i>

00:01:49.601 --> 00:01:51.893
<i>about beyond several decades
or even further,</i>

00:01:51.893 --> 00:01:55.170
have been using these things
called camera traps.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.646
<i>This is a motion-triggered camera.</i>

00:01:57.646 --> 00:02:01.850
The animal walks by,
and the camera takes a series of images.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.570
[camera clicks]

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:07.524
So one of the challenges--
the data is siloed.

00:02:07.524 --> 00:02:12.743
Oftentimes, it's housed
in universities or research institutions.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.590
And there isn't an easy way
to share that data.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.566
The second problem is
it's really hard to process.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:24.202
Like I said, you can be
drowning in all of this data,

00:02:24.202 --> 00:02:25.918
millions and millions of images,

00:02:25.918 --> 00:02:29.230
and what you really need
is the species of your interest.

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:32.820
So we have been working with
the Google Cloud platform

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:36.150
to upload, to manage, and share, and store

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.090
all of this camera trap data,
millions and millions of images

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.350
on the Google Cloud.

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:45.030
And then, we've been applying
artificial intelligence models

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:49.550
to understand, make predictions
upon what species is in what image.

00:02:50.515 --> 00:02:52.620
This project is quite amazing.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.940
And now, I will walk you through
how to build a simple version.

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.723
Let's start with the main ingredients

00:02:57.723 --> 00:03:00.007
for the hardware and the software.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.060
From a hardware standpoint,
we need a camera trap

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:06.540
taking photos that's connected
to a microcontroller,

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.227
like a Raspberry Pi or Arduino.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.669
This basically serves
as a small Linux computer,

00:03:12.669 --> 00:03:15.250
and will have an ML model we install

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:19.237
that labels images of species
and omits blank images.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.130
If it is able to connect to the internet
via a cellular network,

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:25.590
it can send labeled photos automatically.

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:29.400
But in remote areas,
you can carry the microcontroller

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.000
to a Wi-Fi-enabled area periodically
to do this transfer.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:35.446
As a friendly tip,

00:03:35.446 --> 00:03:38.600
when managing a large fleet
of microcontrollers,

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:42.205
it's handy to write a script 
that uses Cloud IoT core

00:03:42.205 --> 00:03:45.890
in order to update all
of your devices simultaneously,

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:48.990
rather than updating each device manually,

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:51.938
especially as the classification
model is updated

00:03:51.938 --> 00:03:54.126
with new data from the field.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:57.281
Next is the software,
which we will be using

00:03:57.281 --> 00:03:59.870
cloud computing resources
from Google Cloud.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:04.198
I think one of the biggest challenges
for this project

00:04:04.798 --> 00:04:10.720
is building it upon a robust enough
platform that it can scale,

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.650
and also that can handle
the kind of inference

00:04:14.382 --> 00:04:17.497
that we'll be doing
when you have biologists

00:04:17.497 --> 00:04:19.968
from all over the world
that are uploading their data

00:04:19.968 --> 00:04:23.453
and using cloud AI Platform predictions.

00:04:23.813 --> 00:04:25.620
For this project, specifically,

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:29.250
the estimated cost per month
is less than $30,

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:30.837
as of the making of this video,

00:04:30.837 --> 00:04:33.202
and includes three gigabytes of images.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.840
There are two main workflows
in the solution--

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:40.527
one is a training model,
and the other is classifying images.

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:44.297
And the two products
that perform most of the heavy lifting

00:04:44.297 --> 00:04:46.660
is Dataflow and AI Platform.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:48.522
Let's start with Dataflow,

00:04:48.522 --> 00:04:50.974
which is a serverless
data-processing service

00:04:50.974 --> 00:04:53.640
that can process
very large amounts of data

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:56.100
needed for machine learning activities.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:59.156
In this scenario,
we use it to run two jobs.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:02.840
The first job creates
a database in BigQuery

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:06.170
by uploading two columns
of the metadata

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:09.744
from the camera trap data set
from <i>lila.science.</i>

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:12.350
This is a one-time setup.

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:15.740
Those two columns contain the category
of the label of the species.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:20.060
The other is the file's name
or the path where the image is located.

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:24.357
It also omits rows that do not contain
relevant information,

00:05:24.357 --> 00:05:27.050
which is an essential data
preprocessing step.

00:05:27.763 --> 00:05:31.283
The second Dataflow job
makes a list of images

00:05:31.283 --> 00:05:34.550
that would be great
for creating a balanced data set.

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:38.205
This is informed by our requirements
for selecting images

00:05:38.205 --> 00:05:42.840
that have a minimum and a maximum
amount of species per category,

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.803
to ensure that we train a bias-free model

00:05:45.803 --> 00:05:49.722
that doesn't include a species
it has too little information about

00:05:49.722 --> 00:05:52.190
and is later unable
to classify it correctly.

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:54.230
When this assessment is complete,

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:57.530
Dataflow proceeds
to download the actual images

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:01.550
into Cloud Storage from that
<i>lila.science</i> data set I mentioned.

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:04.675
This enables us to only store and process

00:06:04.675 --> 00:06:08.053
the images that are relevant
and not the entire data set.

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:11.449
This keeps computational time
and cost down.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.874
Next, we build a model with AI Platform,

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:18.904
which uses AutoML and the images
we store in Cloud Storage.

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:22.999
Once the model is deployed,
we can classify images.

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:26.305
Let's see what the model thinks
about some of these images.

00:06:27.026 --> 00:06:30.021
♪ [music] ♪

00:06:42.842 --> 00:06:44.142
This is fantastic!

00:06:44.142 --> 00:06:46.095
We have a model working.

00:06:46.095 --> 00:06:49.970
Now, the next step is to upload it
to each microcontroller

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:52.432
so that it classifies images locally.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:56.044
Next time you want to upload
these classified images from the field,

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.093
we can save them as JPEG files
directly into Cloud Storage

00:07:00.305 --> 00:07:02.717
and their metadata into BigQuery.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.723
Note that you can optionally
collect more metadata information

00:07:06.723 --> 00:07:10.571
such as the camera trap's
location, the date, etc.,

00:07:11.011 --> 00:07:12.734
and build a simple dashboard

00:07:12.734 --> 00:07:16.007
that you can report
aggregated insights of species.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:21.047
To recap, <i>WildlifeInsights.org</i>
is an amazing project.

00:07:21.047 --> 00:07:23.313
And today, we took apart this case study

00:07:23.313 --> 00:07:26.323
in order to inspire you
to try to replicate the solution

00:07:26.323 --> 00:07:27.892
for your own use case.

00:07:28.116 --> 00:07:29.570
So what's next?

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:32.680
Well, I have appended
in this video's description

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:35.972
several helpful links,
including a link to a notebook

00:07:35.972 --> 00:07:39.062
that contains all the code
we used for this example,

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:41.913
which means you don't have
to write any of it.

00:07:42.375 --> 00:07:45.568
All you have to do is
click <i>Play</i> for each section.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:49.072
And finally, you can hit <i>Like</i>
or leave us a comment

00:07:49.072 --> 00:07:50.740
if you found this content helpful,

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:54.467
and share what other AI for Good
use cases you have in mind.

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:57.500
Thank you so much for watching, friends.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:00.756
I'll leave you with Tanya's
fun fact about this project.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:01.870
Cheers.

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:04.330
A fun fact about working on this project

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:07.391
has been putting a camera trap
in my very own backyard.

00:08:07.391 --> 00:08:09.550
<i>Yeah, we've caught all sorts of animals.</i>

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:13.600
<i>We've caught mountain lions,
bobcats, foxes, deer,</i>

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:15.300
<i>of course, opossums.</i>

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:18.113
And, yeah, it's really great
to be able to teach my kids

00:08:18.113 --> 00:08:20.470
about the wildlife in our backyard.

00:08:21.069 --> 00:08:24.070
♪ [music] ♪

